> Google isn't a newsreader, it's an archive..

Which happens to allow people to read the news.  (Actually, it's a search
engine, which happens to include a usenet archive which people can read.)  A
similar argument can be made that Outlook isn't a newsreader, it's an e-mail
client that supports usenet news; similarly,
Netscape/Mozilla/Firefox/whatever isn't a newsreader, it's a web browser
which happens to include the ability to read usenet data.

That's all, in my opinion, semantics.

If you took up a poll, asking what newsreader people used, I'm sure that
you'd have to list google (or expect it to be written in a lot), simply
because many people use it as their sole way of viewing the usenet news
feeds and posting new comments.  (I started out using it, since I didn't
have a news server through my ISP.)

In short, to argue that a particular system (which includes the ability to
read from and post to the usenet) isn't a newsreader simply because it's not
designed solely and specifically for that purpose is the same level of
argument as declaring that emacs isn't an editor because of all the other
stuff it does [*].  And, as we all know, the vi/emacs debates are some of
the most heated, pointless debates out there.


* [Note: yes, I'm a vi user, but I'm willing to acknowledge that emacs is an
editor...just not a very good one. :) ]



